Hybrid Magnetic Suspension for Two-Wheeled Road Vehicles: Continuous Load Transfer, Fault-Tolerant Control, and Architectural Feasibility

ouadi Maakoul and Deepseek and chatGpt and Claude 

Abstract

This paper establishes the mathematical foundations for a hybrid magnetic suspension architecture applied to two-wheeled road vehicles, in which pneumatic wheels are preserved and a continuously variable magnetic load-transfer mechanism governs the vertical support distribution. Unlike conventional Maglev systems that replace the wheel entirely, the proposed architecture introduces a state variable \lambda(t) \in [0,1) that governs the fraction of vehicle weight carried magnetically. We derive the coupled force–moment allocation between front and rear magnetic modules with independent wheel and actuator coordinates, correcting sign errors that can produce negative wheel loads in physically realistic parameter regimes. The fault-tolerant requirement is formalized as a constrained viability problem in which actuator failure must drive the system toward a wheel-supported invariant set while preserving F_{W,i} \geq F_{\min} throughout the transition. A control barrier function formulation is developed for the wheel-load constraint, with the constraint relative degree determined by the actuator dynamics. A fail-safe magnetic flux shunt with passive spring-return is identified as a candidate enabler for fault-tolerant permanent-magnet control, and the post-fault admissibility condition is reduced to a nonlinear-spring equilibrium inequality. The friction ellipse constraint is shown to bind before wheel lift-off in combined longitudinal–lateral manoeuvres. Technical specifications are provided for a 500 kg reference quadricycle as a vertical/pitch subproblem validation platform, with the two-wheeled vehicle treated as a subsequent dynamic stress case including roll-stability constraints.

---

1. Introduction

1.1 Background

Magnetic levitation has developed along two principal architectural lines: electromagnetic suspension (EMS), in which attractive forces from current-carrying coils support the vehicle, and electrodynamic suspension (EDS), in which repulsive forces arise from induced currents in a conducting track. The Transrapid and the Japanese SCMaglev represent the mature engineering expressions of these principles. More recently, permanent-magnet electrodynamic suspension (PMEDS) has been integrated into a road vehicle, and the Inductrack concept has demonstrated passive levitation above a transition speed of a few kilometres per hour.

These systems share a common structural assumption: the magnetic force is treated as a binary support mechanism. The vehicle either levitates or rests on its wheels. The Inductrack is explicitly fail-safe in the sense that drive system failure results in the vehicle slowing down and settling onto auxiliary wheels at low speed. The PMEDS car uses its own wheels and engine to provide take-off speed, again treating levitation as a threshold phenomenon.

1.2 Problem Statement

This paper investigates the continuous, controlled transfer of vertical load between a magnetic suspension and the pneumatic wheels of a two-wheeled road vehicle. The central variable is the magnetic load fraction

\lambda(t) = \frac{F_M(t)}{mg}, \qquad 0 \leq \lambda < 1

where F_M is the total magnetic force and mg is the vehicle weight. The conventional vehicle corresponds to \lambda = 0; full magnetic support to \lambda \to 1. The regime 0 < \lambda < 1 is the object of study.

1.3 Infrastructure Requirement

A first-order architectural constraint must be stated explicitly: on plain asphalt, permanent-magnet, electromagnetic, and electrodynamic mechanisms produce no vertical force against the road surface. A magnetic road vehicle therefore requires either (i) a ferromagnetic reaction rail for EMS attractive force, (ii) a conductive track or sheet for EDS/PMEDS repulsive force, or (iii) a combination. The architecture applies to equipped roads, not arbitrary roads. The Inductrack concept has been proposed with “the Litz loops or laminated track submerged below the road surface,” which would make the road “equally suitable for conventional vehicles and maglev road vehicles” . This infrastructure requirement is not merely economic—it defines the deployment domain and must be stated before any feasibility claim.

1.4 Contributions

The contribution of this paper is sixfold. First, it derives the coupled force–moment allocation with independent wheel and actuator coordinates, with a sign convention that guarantees non-negative wheel loads at \lambda = 0. Second, it formulates the friction-ellipse constraint, showing that traction and braking limits bind before wheel lift-off. Third, it establishes a parametric mass feasibility relation using a system-level lift-to-mass ratio R_{\rm sys} that includes structural, power, cooling, and shunt masses, not magnet mass alone. Fourth, it reduces the post-fault admissibility condition to a nonlinear-spring equilibrium inequality that is simpler than the general viability argument. Fifth, it develops a control barrier function formulation for the wheel-load constraint with relative degree determined by the actuator dynamics. Sixth, it sharpens the novelty claim to a single leading proposition: fault-triggered load handover with a positive-load invariant.

1.5 Paper Structure

Section 2 establishes the corrected mathematical foundations. Section 3 derives the friction ellipse and traction constraints. Section 4 develops the PM nonlinear-spring model and the post-fault admissibility condition. Section 5 formulates the fail-safe shunt and fault transition. Section 6 develops the control barrier function formulation. Section 7 derives the system mass feasibility relation. Section 8 presents architecture and technical specifications. Section 9 assesses novelty. Section 10 defines the research program.

