4. PM Nonlinear Spring and Post-Fault Admissibility

4.1 The PM as a Nonlinear Spring

In the attractive configuration, the permanent-magnet force increases as the gap decreases:

F_{PM}(e) = \frac{B_g(e)^2 A_{\rm eff}}{2\mu_0}

with B_g(e) increasing as e decreases. This is a nonlinear spring with negative stiffness: \partial F_{PM}/\partial e < 0 for attractive PM.

Post-fault, the gap is set by wheel contact:

e_{\rm contact} = e_0 - z_{\rm wheel}

where z_{\rm wheel} is the wheel deflection under load.

4.2 Post-Fault Admissibility Condition

The clean admissibility condition for the post-fault equilibrium is:

\boxed{
F_{PM}(e_{\rm contact}) \leq mg - 2F_{\min}
}

This condition ensures that the residual PM force, at the gap determined by wheel contact, does not unload the wheels below F_{\min}. It is simpler than the general viability argument and can be stated as the first theorem.

Theorem 1 (Post-fault admissibility). A permanent-magnet bias is admissible for fault-tolerant operation if and only if the PM force at the wheel-contact gap satisfies

F_{PM}(e_{\rm contact}) \leq mg - 2F_{\min}

Proof. At the post-fault equilibrium with wheels in contact, the vertical equilibrium is

F_{PM}(e_{\rm contact}) + F_{W,f} + F_{W,r} = mg

The wheel-load constraint requires F_{W,f} \geq F_{\min} and F_{W,r} \geq F_{\min}, hence F_{W,f} + F_{W,r} \geq 2F_{\min}. Substituting:

F_{PM}(e_{\rm contact}) \leq mg - 2F_{\min}

as required. ∎

4.3 Implication for PM Sizing

The PM bias must be designed so that its force at the wheel-contact gap does not exceed mg - 2F_{\min}. This is a design constraint on the PM geometry, magnetization, and shunt configuration. If the PM cannot be shunted sufficiently, the architecture is inadmissible.

