7. System Mass Feasibility

7.1 System-Level Ratio

The magnet-only ratio R_{L/M} = F_M/(m_M g) is not the appropriate design metric. The system-level ratio is

R_{\rm sys} = \frac{F_M}{m_{\rm mag,sys} g}

where

m_{\rm mag,sys} = m_{\rm PM} + m_{\rm EMS} + m_{\rm shunt} + m_{\rm power} + m_{\rm cooling} + m_{\rm structure}

For \lambda = 0.5 and a 500 kg vehicle, the required magnetic force is F_M = 0.5 \times 500 \times 9.81 = 2452 N. If the magnetic system mass is 63–98 kg, then

R_{\rm sys} = \frac{2452}{(63 \text{ to } 98) \times 9.81} \approx \frac{2452}{618 \text{ to } 961} \approx 2.5 \text{ to } 4.0

This is the correct feasibility metric. The historical Inductrack ratio of 10–40 refers to magnet mass only and does not include the auxiliary systems.

7.2 Parametric Formulation

Let

r = \frac{m_{\rm mag,sys}}{m_0}

where m_0 is the non-magnetic vehicle mass. The total mass is m = m_0(1+r). The magnetic force requirement is

F_M = \lambda m g = \lambda m_0(1+r)g

By definition of R_{\rm sys}:

m_{\rm mag,sys} g R_{\rm sys} = \lambda m_0(1+r)g

r m_0 g R_{\rm sys} = \lambda m_0(1+r)g

r R_{\rm sys} = \lambda(1+r)

r(R_{\rm sys} - \lambda) = \lambda

\boxed{
r = \frac{m_{\rm mag,sys}}{m_0} = \frac{\lambda}{R_{\rm sys} - \lambda}
}

7.3 Feasibility Condition

A finite positive solution for m_{\rm mag,sys} requires

\boxed{
R_{\rm sys} > \lambda
}

If R_{\rm sys} \leq \lambda, no finite magnetic system mass can satisfy the force requirement within the assumed system ratio. For \lambda = 0.5 and R_{\rm sys} = 3, the mass ratio is

r = \frac{0.5}{3 - 0.5} = 0.2

giving m_{\rm mag,sys} = 100 kg for m_0 = 500 kg. This is a realistic estimate for a complete magnetic system including power, cooling, and structure.

7.4 Self-Consistent Mass Budget

The mass budget must be solved self-consistently. The effective non-magnetic mass is

m_{0,\rm eff} = m_0 + m_{\rm aux}

where m_{\rm aux} includes EMS, shunt, power, cooling, and structure masses that are not part of the magnetic force-generating system. Then

m_{\rm mag,sys} = \frac{\lambda}{R_{\rm sys} - \lambda} \, m_{0,\rm eff}

For m_0 = 500 kg, m_{\rm aux} = 50–85 kg, R_{\rm sys} = 3, and \lambda = 0.5:

m_{\rm mag,sys} = \frac{0.5}{2.5} \times (550 \text{ to } 585) = 110 \text{ to } 117 \text{ kg}

Total vehicle mass: 660–702 kg.

