8.3 EMS Design Constraints

For dynamic control, the EMS force requirement is determined by the disturbance spectrum, not by the total magnetic force. If the PM bias carries the static load, the EMS only needs to supply disturbance rejection and transient correction. For a road-class B disturbance at v = 10 m/s, the required EMS force bandwidth is approximately 5–10 Hz, and the force amplitude is on the order of 200–500 N.

At an air gap of 15 mm with a pole area of 0.02 m², the required gap flux density for 500 N is

B_g = \sqrt{\frac{2\mu_0 F}{A}} = \sqrt{\frac{2 \cdot 4\pi \times 10^{-7} \cdot 500}{0.02}} \approx 0.25 \text{ T}

This is substantially lower than the 0.55 T required for 2.45 kN and is within the range of conventional electromagnetic actuator designs. The feasibility for the proposed geometry must be established by electromagnetic finite-element analysis including saturation, fringing, thermal limits, and force-gap sensitivity.

8.4 Road Disturbance Model

The road profile is modelled as a zero-mean, stationary Gaussian random process using the power spectral density approach prescribed in ISO 8608 :

G_d(n) = G_d(n_0) \left(\frac{n}{n_0}\right)^{-w}

where n is the spatial frequency in cycles per metre, n_0 = 0.1 cycle/m is the reference spatial frequency, G_d(n_0) is the roughness coefficient, and w = 2 is the spectral exponent . For Class B (good surfaces), G_d(n_0) = 32 \times 10^{-6} m³ .

The minimum wheel load F_{\min} becomes a chance constraint:

\Pr(F_{W,i} < F_{\min}) \leq p_{\rm fail}

where p_{\rm fail} is the admissible probability of wheel-load violation. This requires the wheel-hop dynamics and tire relaxation to be included in the feasibility analysis.

