9. Novelty Assessment

9.1 Prior Art

The existing literature contains the following elements:

· Magnetic levitation of road vehicles with retained wheels (PMEDS prototype)
· Natural transition from levitation to wheels at low speed (Inductrack)
· Fault-tolerant control of multi-point Maglev levitation systems against partial actuator failures, with experimental validation 
· Hybrid electromagnetic suspension with permanent-magnet bias for power reduction
· Two-wheeled vehicles with magnetic components (magnetic shock absorbers)
· Hybrid magnetic hover and rail systems with wheels maintained in contact for static load accommodation 

9.2 Sharpened Novelty Claim

The five-way conjunction of the original claim is safe but weak. The leading novelty claim is:

\boxed{
\text{Fault-triggered load handover with a positive-load invariant}
}

for a road vehicle, where the magnetic system is required to drive \lambda \to 0 while preserving F_{W,i} \geq F_{\min} throughout the transition. This is the complement of the Maglev fault-tolerant control literature, which preserves levitation by magnetic compensation. The present problem requires magnetic fault abandonment with wheel-load preservation.

A targeted search for “hybrid levitation with auxiliary wheels,” “load-shedding maglev,” and “active magnetic suspension for motorcycles” did not identify a published architecture that formulates this specific problem.

