10. Research Program

10.1 Phase-0 Simulation

A minimal 2-DOF (z, \theta) simulation with PM nonlinear spring, EMS, shunt dynamics, and wheel-load constraints should be constructed. The output is the feasible \lambda region under a step fault. One plot is sufficient to make the paper concrete.

10.2 Central Hypothesis

\boxed{
\exists \, \mathcal{K}_{\rm robust} \neq \varnothing :
\text{mass, energy, stability, and fault constraints are simultaneously satisfiable}
}

If \mathcal{K}_{\rm robust} = \varnothing in the realistic parameter region, the architecture is falsified. If \mathcal{K}_{\rm robust} \neq \varnothing, the control and demonstrator program is justified.

10.3 Falsification Criteria for 10 m Demonstrator

· Achievable \lambda before wheel-load margin F_{W,\min} is violated
· Fault transition time from \lambda_0 to \lambda=0 with F_W > F_{\min} throughout
· Total magnetic system mass vs. force achieved (compare to R_{\rm sys})
· Power consumption at \lambda=0.5 and thermal limits
· For two-wheeled: maximum lean angle at \lambda=0.3 and fault-triggered roll response

10.4 Next Mathematical Step

The next calculation should simultaneously construct:

1. The achievable actuator domain (F_M, M_M) \in \mathcal{A} with polytope constraints
2. The wheel-load constraints F_{W,f}, F_{W,r} \geq F_{\min}
3. The friction ellipse constraint
4. The fault transition dynamics with shunt settling time

This is the next mathematical building block that will determine whether the architecture merits a demonstrator.

