Appendix D: Magnetic Force Models

D.1 Permanent-Magnet Force

D.1.1 General Expression

For a permanent magnet with remanent flux density B_r, coercivity H_c, pole area A_m, magnet length \ell_m, air gap e, and magnetic circuit geometry \mathcal{G}, the PM force is

F_{PM} = f_{PM}(B_r, H_c, A_m, \ell_m, e, \mathcal{G}, s)

where s \in [0,1] is the shunt state.

D.1.2 Idealized Model

For a simple geometry with negligible fringing and saturation, the gap flux density is

B_g = B_r \frac{\ell_m}{\ell_m + \mu_r e}

where \mu_r is the relative permeability of the magnet. The PM force is

F_{PM} = \frac{B_g^2 A_{\rm eff}}{2\mu_0}

Substituting:

F_{PM} = \frac{A_{\rm eff}}{2\mu_0} \left( B_r \frac{\ell_m}{\ell_m + \mu_r e} \right)^2

D.1.3 Gap Dependence

For small gaps e \ll \ell_m/\mu_r:

F_{PM} \approx \frac{B_r^2 A_{\rm eff}}{2\mu_0}

which is independent of gap. For large gaps e \gg \ell_m/\mu_r:

F_{PM} \approx \frac{B_r^2 A_{\rm eff}}{2\mu_0} \left( \frac{\ell_m}{\mu_r e} \right)^2 \propto \frac{1}{e^2}

D.1.4 Shunt Effect

The shunt provides a bypass path for the PM flux. Let the shunt reluctance be \mathcal{R}_s(s) and the working gap reluctance be \mathcal{R}_g(e). The flux divider gives

\frac{\Phi_g}{\Phi_{\rm total}} = \frac{\mathcal{R}_s(s)}{\mathcal{R}_s(s) + \mathcal{R}_g(e)}

The PM force scales as

F_{PM}(e, s) = F_{PM}(e, 0) \left( \frac{\mathcal{R}_s(s)}{\mathcal{R}_s(s) + \mathcal{R}_g(e)} \right)^2

For s = 1 (shunt fully closed), \mathcal{R}_s \to 0 and F_{PM} \to 0. For s = 0 (shunt fully open), \mathcal{R}_s \to \infty and F_{PM} \to F_{PM}(e, 0).

D.2 Electrodynamic Suspension Force

D.2.1 General Expression

For a conductive track with resistivity \rho, conductivity \sigma, thickness t_c, and a magnetic array with peak field B_0 moving at velocity v and gap e:

F_{EDS} = f_{EDS}(v, e, \rho, \sigma, t_c, B_0, \mathcal{G})

D.2.2 Inductrack Model

For the Inductrack configuration with a Halbach array and a laminated track, the lift and drag forces are

F_{\rm lift} = F_0 \frac{(v/v_0)^2}{1 + (v/v_0)^2}

F_{\rm drag} = F_0 \frac{v/v_0}{1 + (v/v_0)^2}

where v_0 is the characteristic speed and F_0 is the asymptotic lift force.

The lift-to-drag ratio is

\frac{F_{\rm lift}}{F_{\rm drag}} = \frac{v}{v_0}

At operating speeds v \gg v_0, the ratio reaches 200:1 or higher.

D.2.3 Characteristic Speed

The characteristic speed is

v_0 = \frac{2\rho t_c}{\mu_0 w}

where w is the track width parameter. For typical laminated copper tracks, v_0 is on the order of a few km/h.

D.3 Electromagnetic Suspension Force

D.3.1 General Expression

For a coil with current I, turns N, gap e, and magnetic circuit geometry \mathcal{G}:

F_{EMS} = f_{EMS}(I, e, \mathcal{G}, \mu, \text{saturation})

D.3.2 Idealized Model

For a simple C-core geometry with negligible saturation:

F_{EMS} = \frac{\mu_0 N^2 A I^2}{4e^2}

This is valid only for the specific geometry and should not be presented as a universal law.

D.3.3 Local Linearization

For small perturbations around an operating point (I_0, e_0):

\Delta F_{EMS} = K_I \Delta I + K_e \Delta e

where

K_I = \frac{\partial F_{EMS}}{\partial I} \bigg|_{I_0, e_0} = \frac{\mu_0 N^2 A I_0}{2e_0^2}

K_e = \frac{\partial F_{EMS}}{\partial e} \bigg|_{I_0, e_0} = -\frac{\mu_0 N^2 A I_0^2}{2e_0^3}

The negative stiffness K_e < 0 is the inherent instability of EMS.

D.3.4 Ampere-Turn Requirement

For a desired flux density B_g over two air gaps of length e:

NI = \frac{B_g \cdot 2e}{\mu_0}

For B_g = 0.55 T and e = 15 mm:

NI = \frac{0.55 \times 2 \times 0.015}{4\pi \times 10^{-7}} \approx 13,100 \text{ A-turns}

This is a substantial excitation requirement, confirming that PM bias is essential for power reduction.

