Appendix F: Post-Fault Admissibility Theorem

F.1 Theorem Statement

Theorem 1 (Post-fault admissibility). A permanent-magnet bias is admissible for fault-tolerant operation if and only if the PM force at the wheel-contact gap satisfies

F_{PM}(e_{\rm contact}) \leq mg - 2F_{\min}

F.2 Proof

Necessity. At the post-fault equilibrium with wheels in contact, the vertical equilibrium is

F_{PM}(e_{\rm contact}) + F_{W,f} + F_{W,r} = mg

The wheel-load constraint requires

F_{W,f} \geq F_{\min}, \qquad F_{W,r} \geq F_{\min}

hence

F_{W,f} + F_{W,r} \geq 2F_{\min}

Substituting:

F_{PM}(e_{\rm contact}) = mg - (F_{W,f} + F_{W,r}) \leq mg - 2F_{\min}

Sufficiency. If F_{PM}(e_{\rm contact}) \leq mg - 2F_{\min}, then the residual wheel load is

F_{W,f} + F_{W,r} = mg - F_{PM}(e_{\rm contact}) \geq 2F_{\min}

A symmetric distribution F_{W,f} = F_{W,r} = (mg - F_{PM})/2 \geq F_{\min} satisfies the constraint. The pitch moment can be adjusted to achieve this distribution if the actuator polytope permits. ∎

F.3 Corollary: Shunt Requirement

If the shunt can reduce the PM force to F_{PM}^{\rm residual} = f_{PM}(e, 1, \mathcal{G}), then the post-fault admissibility condition is

F_{PM}^{\rm residual} \leq mg - 2F_{\min}

This is a design constraint on the shunt effectiveness.

F.4 Corollary: Maximum Admissible PM Force

The maximum admissible PM force at the wheel-contact gap is

F_{PM}^{\max} = mg - 2F_{\min}

For m = 500 kg and F_{\min} = 200 N:

F_{PM}^{\max} = 500 \times 9.81 - 400 = 4505 \text{ N}

This is 92% of the vehicle weight. The PM bias can carry up to 92% of the weight without violating the wheel-load constraint at equilibrium. However, the friction ellipse imposes a stricter bound during manoeuvres.

