Appendix G: Shunt Dynamics and Settling Time

G.1 Shunt Actuator Model

The shunt is modelled as a spring-mass-damper system:

m_s \ddot{s} + c_s \dot{s} + k_s s = F_{\rm actuator}(t)

where s \in [0,1] is the shunt state, m_s is the shunt mass, c_s is the damping coefficient, and k_s is the spring stiffness.

G.2 Passive Spring-Return

For a fail-safe design, the spring drives s \to 1 when the actuator force is zero:

k_s s_{\rm rest} = 0 \implies s_{\rm rest} = 0 \quad \text{(shunt open)}

Wait—for fail-safe, the shunt should close on power loss, so s_{\rm rest} = 1. This requires a spring that drives s \to 1:

m_s \ddot{s} + c_s \dot{s} + k_s (s - 1) = F_{\rm actuator}(t)

G.3 Step Response

For a step change in actuator force from F_0 to 0 at t = 0, the shunt response is

s(t) = 1 - (1 - s_0) e^{-\zeta_s \omega_{n,s} t} \left( \cos(\omega_{d,s} t) + \frac{\zeta_s}{\sqrt{1-\zeta_s^2}} \sin(\omega_{d,s} t) \right)

where

\omega_{n,s} = \sqrt{\frac{k_s}{m_s}}, \qquad \zeta_s = \frac{c_s}{2\sqrt{k_s m_s}}, \qquad \omega_{d,s} = \omega_{n,s} \sqrt{1-\zeta_s^2}

G.4 Settling Time

The settling time to within 2% of the final value is

t_s \approx \frac{4}{\zeta_s \omega_{n,s}}

For \zeta_s = 0.7 and \omega_{n,s} = 100 rad/s:

t_s \approx \frac{4}{0.7 \times 100} = 0.057 \text{ s} = 57 \text{ ms}

G.5 Fault Transition Requirement

The shunt settling time must be shorter than the wheel-load reserve depletion time:

t_s \leq \frac{\Delta F_{W,\rm reserve}}{\dot{F}_{W,\rm inertial}}

For \Delta F_{W,\rm reserve} = 500 N and \dot{F}_{W,\rm inertial} = 10,000 N/s:

t_s \leq 0.05 \text{ s} = 50 \text{ ms}

The shunt design must satisfy t_s \leq 50 ms.

