Appendix I: System Mass Feasibility

I.1 System-Level Ratio

The system-level lift-to-mass ratio is

R_{\rm sys} = \frac{F_M}{m_{\rm mag,sys} g}

where

m_{\rm mag,sys} = m_{\rm PM} + m_{\rm EMS} + m_{\rm shunt} + m_{\rm power} + m_{\rm cooling} + m_{\rm structure}

I.2 Mass Ratio Derivation

Let

r = \frac{m_{\rm mag,sys}}{m_0}

where m_0 is the non-magnetic vehicle mass. The total mass is

m = m_0(1+r)

The magnetic force requirement is

F_M = \lambda m g = \lambda m_0(1+r)g

By definition of R_{\rm sys}:

m_{\rm mag,sys} g R_{\rm sys} = \lambda m_0(1+r)g

Substituting m_{\rm mag,sys} = r m_0:

r m_0 g R_{\rm sys} = \lambda m_0(1+r)g

r R_{\rm sys} = \lambda(1+r)

r(R_{\rm sys} - \lambda) = \lambda

\boxed{
r = \frac{\lambda}{R_{\rm sys} - \lambda}
}

I.3 Feasibility Condition

A finite positive solution for m_{\rm mag,sys} requires

\boxed{
R_{\rm sys} > \lambda
}

If R_{\rm sys} \leq \lambda, no finite magnetic system mass can satisfy the force requirement.

I.4 Self-Consistent Mass Budget

The effective non-magnetic mass is

m_{0,\rm eff} = m_0 + m_{\rm aux}

where m_{\rm aux} includes EMS, shunt, power, cooling, and structure masses that are not part of the magnetic force-generating system.

Then

m_{\rm mag,sys} = \frac{\lambda}{R_{\rm sys} - \lambda} \, m_{0,\rm eff}

I.5 Numerical Example

For m_0 = 500 kg, m_{\rm aux} = 50–85 kg, R_{\rm sys} = 3, and \lambda = 0.5:

m_{0,\rm eff} = 550 \text{ to } 585 \text{ kg}

m_{\rm mag,sys} = \frac{0.5}{3 - 0.5} \times (550 \text{ to } 585) = \frac{0.5}{2.5} \times (550 \text{ to } 585) = 110 \text{ to } 117 \text{ kg}

Total vehicle mass:

m = 550 \text{ to } 585 + 110 \text{ to } 117 = 660 \text{ to } 702 \text{ kg}

I.6 Comparison with Magnet-Only Ratio

If only the magnet mass is considered with R_{L/M} = 20:

m_{\rm PM} = \frac{0.5}{20 - 0.5} \times 550 = \frac{0.5}{19.5} \times 550 = 14.1 \text{ kg}

The magnet-only mass is 14 kg, but the complete system mass is 110–117 kg. The system-level ratio R_{\rm sys} \approx 3 is the correct feasibility metric, not R_{L/M} \approx 20.

