Appendix J: Road Disturbance Model (ISO 8608)

J.1 Power Spectral Density

The road profile is modelled as a zero-mean, stationary Gaussian random process with power spectral density

G_d(n) = G_d(n_0) \left(\frac{n}{n_0}\right)^{-w}

where:

· n is the spatial frequency in cycles/m
· n_0 = 0.1 cycle/m is the reference spatial frequency
· G_d(n_0) is the roughness coefficient
· w = 2 is the spectral exponent for most roads

J.2 Road Classes

Road Class G_d(n_0) (m³) Description
A 16 \times 10^{-6} Very good
B 64 \times 10^{-6} Good
C 256 \times 10^{-6} Average
D 1024 \times 10^{-6} Poor
E 4096 \times 10^{-6} Very poor

J.3 Time-Domain Realization

For a vehicle travelling at speed v, the temporal frequency is

f = v n

The temporal PSD is

G_d(f) = \frac{G_d(n_0)}{v} \left(\frac{f}{v n_0}\right)^{-2}

J.4 Chance Constraint

The minimum wheel load F_{\min} becomes a chance constraint:

\Pr(F_{W,i} < F_{\min}) \leq p_{\rm fail}

For a Gaussian road profile and a linear suspension model, the wheel load is Gaussian with mean \mu_{F_W} and standard deviation \sigma_{F_W}. The chance constraint becomes

\Phi\left(\frac{F_{\min} - \mu_{F_W}}{\sigma_{F_W}}\right) \leq p_{\rm fail}

where \Phi is the standard normal CDF.

For p_{\rm fail} = 0.01:

\frac{F_{\min} - \mu_{F_W}}{\sigma_{F_W}} \leq -2.33

\mu_{F_W} \geq F_{\min} + 2.33 \sigma_{F_W}

J.5 Wheel-Hop Dynamics

The wheel-hop mode is modelled as a quarter-car system:

m_w \ddot{z}_w + c_s (\dot{z}_w - \dot{z}_b) + k_s (z_w - z_b) + k_t (z_w - z_r) = 0

where m_w is the wheel mass, z_w is the wheel displacement, z_b is the body displacement, z_r is the road input, k_s is the suspension stiffness, c_s is the suspension damping, and k_t is the tyre stiffness.

The wheel-hop frequency is

\omega_{\rm hop} = \sqrt{\frac{k_t + k_s}{m_w}}

For typical values k_t = 200,000 N/m, k_s = 20,000 N/m, m_w = 20 kg:

\omega_{\rm hop} = \sqrt{\frac{220,000}{20}} = \sqrt{11,000} = 105 \text{ rad/s} = 16.7 \text{ Hz}

The controller bandwidth must be sufficient to reject disturbances at this frequency.

