Appendix Q: Proof of the \lambda Bound from Wheel-Load Constraints

Q.1 Theorem Statement

Theorem 2 (Wheel-load bound). A necessary condition for simultaneously maintaining F_{W,f} \geq F_{\min} and F_{W,r} \geq F_{\min} with a freely adjustable magnetic pitch moment M_M is

\lambda \leq 1 - \frac{2F_{\min}}{mg}

Q.2 Proof

The admissible interval for M_M is

M_M^{\min} \leq M_M \leq M_M^{\max}

with

M_M^{\min} = m h a_x - b(1-\lambda)mg + L F_{\min}

M_M^{\max} = m h a_x + a(1-\lambda)mg - L F_{\min}

For a solution to exist:

M_M^{\min} \leq M_M^{\max}

Substituting:

m h a_x - b(1-\lambda)mg + L F_{\min} \leq m h a_x + a(1-\lambda)mg - L F_{\min}

The inertial term cancels:

-b(1-\lambda)mg + L F_{\min} \leq a(1-\lambda)mg - L F_{\min}

2L F_{\min} \leq (a+b)(1-\lambda)mg

Since L = a + b:

2L F_{\min} \leq L(1-\lambda)mg

2F_{\min} \leq (1-\lambda)mg

\lambda \leq 1 - \frac{2F_{\min}}{mg}

∎

Q.3 Corollary

If \lambda > 1 - 2F_{\min}/(mg), no pitch moment M_M can simultaneously maintain both wheel loads above F_{\min}. The system must either reduce \lambda or accept a violation of the wheel-load constraint.

---

Appendix R: Detailed Derivation of the System Mass Feasibility

R.1 Derivation

Let m_0 be the non-magnetic vehicle mass and m_{\rm mag,sys} be the total magnetic system mass. The total mass is

m = m_0 + m_{\rm mag,sys} = m_0(1+r)

where

r = \frac{m_{\rm mag,sys}}{m_0}

The magnetic force requirement is

F_M = \lambda m g = \lambda m_0(1+r)g

The system-level lift-to-mass ratio is

R_{\rm sys} = \frac{F_M}{m_{\rm mag,sys} g}

Substituting F_M:

R_{\rm sys} = \frac{\lambda m_0(1+r)g}{m_{\rm mag,sys} g} = \frac{\lambda m_0(1+r)}{m_{\rm mag,sys}}

Since m_{\rm mag,sys} = r m_0:

R_{\rm sys} = \frac{\lambda m_0(1+r)}{r m_0} = \frac{\lambda(1+r)}{r}

Solving for r:

R_{\rm sys} r = \lambda(1+r)

R_{\rm sys} r = \lambda + \lambda r

r(R_{\rm sys} - \lambda) = \lambda

r = \frac{\lambda}{R_{\rm sys} - \lambda}

∎

R.2 Feasibility Condition

For r > 0, we require

R_{\rm sys} - \lambda > 0

R_{\rm sys} > \lambda

If R_{\rm sys} \leq \lambda, no positive finite r satisfies the equation.

R.3 Self-Consistent Extension

If auxiliary masses m_{\rm aux} are not part of the magnetic force-generating system but must be included in the total mass, the effective non-magnetic mass is

m_{0,\rm eff} = m_0 + m_{\rm aux}

Then

m_{\rm mag,sys} = \frac{\lambda}{R_{\rm sys} - \lambda} \, m_{0,\rm eff}

R.4 Numerical Example

For m_0 = 500 kg, m_{\rm aux} = 50 kg, R_{\rm sys} = 3, \lambda = 0.5:

m_{0,\rm eff} = 550 \text{ kg}

m_{\rm mag,sys} = \frac{0.5}{2.5} \times 550 = 110 \text{ kg}

m = 550 + 110 = 660 \text{ kg}

